ldn't get them very far because  language is two-way communication and if  you can say something and you don't  understand what people are saying to you  then it's not much use so take away the  obligation or the illusion that they're  going to learn to speak quickly and say  your task is to understand the language  and to notice what's happening so one  thing that is for sure is that just as  with the jigsaw puzzle if you were given  three or four or five big pieces to the  jigsaw puzzle some of the smaller pieces  might start to make sense to you but  well you don't have anything in front of  you not even really a picture because  you have no sense of this language all  the little pieces all the grammatical  explanations all the tables of endings  none of these things have any lasting  effect you can study them until your  eyes get tired and they aren't gonna  have much impact and I notice this for  example in Russian you know the endings  for the say even in the nominative  nominative plural you know there's  different e ending some is that funny  ly some is the e I never paid much  attention to it I read all the  explanations soft consonants hard  consonants no lasting impact but when  enough of the other pieces are in place  now you're left with a few little  tidbits tidbits which often seem to be  easy to explain but which which simply  don't stick they don't stick until later  on so the first thing to understand is  that the things that we can notice when  we start are different from the things  that we will notice later on and the  more we have things in place the more we  notice so how can we get students to  notice or how can we train ourselves to  notice well I think if I were teaching a  language I would begin by pointing out  only the most obvious things in a  general way and either reading with the  class or if we had a screen and we could  maybe use different highlighting for  certain aspects of the language for  example it's enough to say that in  French there is gender notice that there  is a la fine  notice that the if you wanted to get to  the point where say to tell them there  is agreement but never with the idea  that they have to remember it and they  have to remember which verb which nouns  are masculine in which you're feminine  they aren't going to remember those  things  similarly in tense saying you know there  are different tenses in Romance  languages in English talk about the  generalities in Japanese they have these  little you know what talk she wah  another little comma after the the you  know subject of a sentence or a not an e  like the wah and the need a indicate the  direction of you know it's sort of like  a preposition that comes  after the Mount all of these things can  be pointed out in a very general way I  say but don't worry about that  I just pointing out a few things and we  want you to read and listen and try to  accumulate words and and get some sense  of what the little story is about that's  the other thing I said in my previous  video that if you see a list or let's  say ten sentences that all contain a  particular pattern that that can help  you notice but that's only true after  you've had a lot of exposure to the  language I realized on on reconsidering  because with you begin with that it's  just too much it doesn't mean anything  you've explained to you that there's a  dative case you kind of vaguely  understand that that happens but but you  have no real feel for it  so you still have to begin with simple  stories so that at least the story  however dumb the story is twenty-second  little story hello how are you what's  your name it has some sense there is  something there that people can can  identify with because they can  understand that the that sort of also  has meaning in my language those is it  this is how they say it in that language  listen to it listen to it say words and  and maybe have it pointed out that there  are some things happening there for  example 