re you struggle to find a word but at  the beginning you struggle so much that  it's just overwhelming you can't give  people too much to notice at first you  have to build up a base so that a few  things stick out and those things might  be different  in the first three months second three  months third three months and so forth  so cultivating this ability to notice  and then later on as you're speaking you  may want to record your voice and to  listen to how you sound versus how the  native speaker sounds but again I  wouldn't do that until you're well along  because initially trying to make the  sounds of the new language is simply too  difficult I wouldn't attempt to and I my  experience I don't attempt to pronounce  until I've had a lot of exposure to the  language so I feel that you know if  teachers could somehow reduce the  expectations that either learners make  up themselves or that we make up the  learners in other words the expectation  that they're going to speak they're  going to speak correctly they're going  to be able to apply these grammar rules  I think those are all unrealistic  expectations if the expectation is  you're going to learn to understand the  language you're going to  and get to where you can comfortably  listen and read and and understand more  and more of the language because you're  going to increase your vocabulary and  along the way you are going to take the  initiative and starting to notice the  patterns and as a teacher we can help  you we can spend the day on or a lesson  on a particular pattern pointing out  where it occurs maybe I have it on a  screen with some highlighting pointing  out these patterns pointing out  relationships between words eventually  looking at word lists that the students  have have gleaned from the reading and  listening and identifying some of the  relationships in terms of patterns and  so on and of course focusing then  eventually on specific patterns and then  doing it in different ways that might  involve competition between groups of  students or between individual students  but all in a light-hearted way and with  the understanding that that the any  correction is any sort of wrong answer  is not going to detract from their mark  it's not it's not a black mark against  them it's it's simply a step in the  process of increasing our ability to  notice what's happening in the language  so I kind of rambled a bit and I  probably not have been as clear as I  would like but I believe these  disability cultivating the ability to  notice is at the core of the art of  language learning so that if we put in  the time if we have a positive attitude  and we can notice what's happening we  will eventually learn to speak and I  have I know this is a fact from my  Russian studies most recently where I  spent I would said these two years  without speaking and I haven't spoken  very much last night I was at a dinner  with 20 Russian people here a Russian  language meetup in Vancouver and it was  a little difficult because five  conversations were going you know across  each other and I couldn't hear so  clearly it's not like listening to the  radio where you were you know the person  is just talking to you but I was doing  fine I was quite able to you know carry  on my hold up my end of the conversation  have not having spoken very much at all  so if you devote yourself to  comprehension if you devote yourself to  noticing the brain will  on these patterns the brain will do it  you have to cross the brain but you can  help it along with doing things that  help you notice or conversely the  teacher can help you by helping you to  notice and in that sense again I'm not  in agreement with the extreme crashing  dice to believe you just do a lot of  extensive reading reading content that's  95% you know and consisting of known  words I just think that takes far far  too long and I think there are things  that we can do to help you notice the  language and also to help you to